    "spec_82": {
        "statement": "Based on the given pages from the MPU-6000/MPU-6050 specification document, answer the following questions concerning the I2C interface.",
        "question": [
            "Which of the following is the correct sequence for starting communication in an I2C protocol? (A) STOP condition -> Address -> START condition, (B) START condition -> Address -> STOP condition, (C) START condition -> Address -> DATA -> STOP condition",
            "What action must a slave device take if it cannot transmit or receive another byte of data?",
            "Calculate the number of bytes transmitted if the master writes a complete single-byte write sequence followed by a burst write sequence of 4 data bytes. Provide the total number of bytes transferred including acknowledgments."
        ],
        "question_type": [
            "single",
            "open",
            "blank"
        ],
        "answer": [
            "C",
            "If a slave cannot transmit or receive another byte of data, it can hold the SCL line LOW, thus forcing the master into a wait state.",
            "15"
        ],
        "explanation": [
            "In the I2C protocol, communication starts with a START condition, followed by the address of the device, then data transfer, and ends with a STOP condition.",
            "The slave device holds SCL LOW when unable to process more data, keeping the master in a wait state to prevent data overflow or communication errors.",
            "In the single-byte write sequence, 7 bytes are transferred (1 start, 1 address, 1 data, 1 stop, and 3 ACKs). In the burst write sequence for 4 data bytes, a total of 8 bytes (4 data and 4 ACKs) are transferred. Therefore, the total is 7 + 8 = 15."
        ],
